%[text] %[text:anchor:T_0B83894C] # Design LLC Resonant Converter
%[text] This design solution demonstrates how to design and analyze the performance of an LLC resonant DC-DC converter. The LLC resonant converter is a soft-switching topology that achieves turn-on zero-voltage switching (ZVS) for the primary MOSFETs and zero-current switching (ZCS) for the secondary diodes. This topology enables high power density, reduced switching losses, and lower electromagnetic interference (EMI). Recent advancements in semiconductor technology and high-power materials allow for higher switching frequencies, which reduces the size of the magnetic components. Soft-switching operation further decreases switching losses and enhances efficiency.
%[text] The model uses the LLC Converter plant block from `ConverterLibrary`. You configure the block through its mask, which sets the model fidelity, the rectifier topology, and the series-inductor realization. The mask also selects the device fidelity, either ideal or thermal MOSFETs and either ideal or tabulated diodes. The same block serves both fast control-design simulation and detailed loss and efficiency analysis.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A6556DB6] ## Design Solution Scope
%[text] This design solution outlines the steps required to translate converter design specifications into a validated design. The workflow uses the first harmonic approximation (FHA) to estimate the resonant tank parameters, plots the gain curve for various load resistances, quality factors, and inductance ratios, and provides guidance on linearizing the converter and tuning the controller. It then summarizes the switching and conduction losses for each semiconductor device and estimates the overall efficiency of the converter.
%[text] In this design solution, you:
%[text] - Estimate and tune the resonant tank parameters
%[text] - Configure the library LLC converter block at the design point
%[text] - Simulate the converter and inspect the resonant-tank voltage and current waveforms
%[text] - Estimate power loss and efficiency \
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B6482FCD] ## System Overview
%[text] The LLC resonant full-bridge DC-DC converter model includes four key components:
%[text] - Full-bridge inverter with SiC MOSFET devices
%[text] - Resonant tank comprising a capacitor and a transformer with specified leakage and magnetizing inductance
%[text] - Full-bridge diode rectifier
%[text] - LLC controller  \
%[text] This figure shows the top-level model. The power circuit is the masked `ConverterLibrary` LLC block, and its icon shows the converter it contains: the full-bridge inverter, the resonant tank realized through the transformer leakage inductance, and the full-bridge diode rectifier. The numbered links under the diagram open this script, the resonant-tank estimator and the controller-design script; open the plant block parameters and the device model that is currently active; switch the primary and secondary device fidelity; and explore the results in the Simscape Results Explorer.
%[text] Open the model:
  %[control:button:6d7c]{"position":[1,2]}
modelName = "LLCResonantConverterFullBridge";
open_system(modelName);
%[text] ![](text:image:75bd)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_72e5] ## Select LLC Resonant Tank Parameters
%[text] To design and tune the LLC converter, use the `EstimateLLCConverterResonantTankParameters` live code. In this workflow, you specify your design requirements and configure the resonant tank; the live code plots the gain curve of the resonant tank using the FHA method.
%[text] To open the LLC resonant converter design workflow, run the following command in the MATLAB® Command Window:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] edit 'EstimateLLCConverterResonantTankParameters'
%[text] ```
if ~exist('LLCDesign', 'var') || ~exist('designTable', 'var')
    LLCConverterDefaultInputParameters;
end
disp(table(designTable,'VariableNames',"LLC Converter Estimated Design Parameters")); %[output:5a3d1c07]
%[text] The plant block mask reads these estimated values straight out of the `LLCDesign` struct, so re-running the estimator updates the model:
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_selectcomp] ## Select Converter Components
%[text] This design solution assists you in selecting appropriate ratings for the primary MOSFET and the secondary diode, and in picking catalog parts that meet those ratings.
%[text] To open the LLC converter component selection workflow, run:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] edit 'SelectLLCConverterComponents'
%[text] ```
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_designcontroller] ## Design the Controller
%[text] This design solution helps you to design and tune two-pole, two-zero Compensator. 
%[text] To open the controller design workflow, run:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] edit 'LLCResonantConverterControllerDesign'
%[text] ```
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_circuit] ## LLC Resonant Converter Circuit
%[text] The LLC resonant converter plant containing the primary full-bridge inverter, the resonant tank, and the secondary diode-bridge rectifier. Choose the model fidelity through the block mask. For faster simulation during controller tuning, use the ideal semiconductor switch and the ideal diode. To estimate switching losses and efficiency, select the `MOSFET (Ideal, Switching) with Thermal` primary device and the `Tabulated Diode` secondary device.
%[text] To open the LLC converter power circuit, run:
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] open_system(strcat(modelName,"/LLC Converter Power Circuit"));
%[text] ```
%[text] ![](text:image:417e)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_sim] ## Define the Operating Point
%[text] Specify the input DC voltage, output reference voltage, and load power. The same operating point drives both the waveform (IV-curve) plots and the loss/efficiency estimate.
  %[control:button:9c1a]{"position":[1,2]}
voltageReference = simscape.Value(360,"V");   % Output reference voltage (V)
inputDCVoltage   = simscape.Value(380,"V");   % Input DC voltage (V)
outputLoadPower  = simscape.Value(7000,"W");  % DC output power (W)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_plot] ### Plot LLC Converter Voltage and Current (IV Curves)
%[text] Plot the converter's voltage and current waveforms using `getConverterWaveforms MATLAB function`. It configures the plant to fast ideal device fidelity, simulates at the operating point, and plots the registry-defined signal pairs - inverter/secondary voltage, primary/secondary current, and input/output DC current.
  %[control:button:4fef]{"position":[1,2]}
llcSimulationData = getConverterWaveforms(modelName, Converter="LLC", ... %[output:group:55749999] %[output:21b4f9e1]
    Vref=voltageReference, Vin=inputDCVoltage, Pload=outputLoadPower, ... %[output:21b4f9e1]
    FrequencyHz=LLCSpec.resonantFrequency.value, FocusCycles=5); %[output:group:55749999] %[output:21b4f9e1]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_loss] ### Estimate Power Loss and Efficiency
%[text] Estimate switching and conduction losses and overall efficiency with `getConverterPowerLoss` MATLAB function. It configures the plant MOSFET fidelity with MOSFET (ideal, Switching) with thermal port and Diode with tabulated diode.
%[text] Losses are averaged over the last 20% of the simulation by default (steady state) to avoid start-up transients; you can set `AveragingWindow` parameter to modify it. You can set `SwitchingLossDetail="separated"` to break switching loss into turn-on, turn-off, and reverse-recovery components.
  %[control:button:55d7]{"position":[1,2]}
simulationTemperature = simscape.Value(85,"degC");   % Device junction temperature for the loss estimation
lossData = getConverterPowerLoss(modelName, Converter="LLC", ... %[output:group:9db183f9] %[output:882635af] %[output:6e495bfb]
    Vref=voltageReference, Vin=inputDCVoltage, Pload=outputLoadPower, ... %[output:882635af] %[output:6e495bfb]
    SimTime=simscape.Value(0.01,"s"), SwitchingLossDetail="lumped", ... %[output:882635af] %[output:6e495bfb]
    SimulationTemperature=simulationTemperature); %[output:group:9db183f9] %[output:882635af] %[output:6e495bfb]
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7A3E9C04] ## Conclusion
%[text] This live code, supporting scripts and models have shown how MATLAB® scripting and Simscape Electrical™ can be used to design an LLC resonant full-bridge converter. Steps covered include sizing of the key passive components to meet converter requirements, modeling the circuit at lower fidelity to support fast simulation and control design, and modeling the circuit with sufficient detail to predict losses and efficiency. For further information on LLC resonant converter design, please consult the list of referenced papers.
%[text] ## References
%[text] 1. Sam Abdel-Rahman, "Resonant LLC Converter:Operation and Design 250W, 33Vin, 400Vout, Design Example", Infineon Technologies North America (IFNA) Corp.
%[text] 2. P. P. Gupta, N. Kumar and U. Nangia, "Design and Analysis of LLC Resonant Converter and CCCV Topology for Battery Charging," *2022 2nd Asian Conference on Innovation in Technology (ASIANCON)*, Ravet, India, 2022, pp. 1-5, doi: 10.1109/ ASIANCON55314.2022.9909135.
%[text] 3. Christophe Basso, "Control Methods of LLC Converters", Future Electronics, 12th Power Analysis and Design Symposium.
%[text] 4. B. Li, X. Zhang, D. Zhou, S. Sun, Y. Wang and D. Xu, "Soft-switching Characteristics Analysis Based on LLC Resonant Converter," *2018 IEEE Transportation Electrification Conference and Expo, Asia-Pacific (ITEC Asia-Pacific)*, Bangkok, Thailand, 2018, pp. 1-6, doi: 10.1109/ITEC-AP.2018.8433301.
%[text] 5. Dhaval Patel and Ramesh Kankanala, "Digital Compensator Design for LLC Resonant Converter", Application note (AN1477), Microchip Technology Inc. \
